 "summary": "## Security analysis of Jinja2 3.1.5 (src/jinja2, 24 files, ~10.9k LOC)\n\n### Overview\nJinja2 is a template engine; the security model is \"templates are trusted code, *data* is untrusted\". `SandboxedEnvironment` is offered as a restricted subset for semi-trusted template authors. Sink inventory + AST/data-flow probing identified the following risk surfaces.\n\n### Findings\n\n**1. [Critical / CWE-502] Unsafe deserialization of bytecode cache — `src/jinja2/bccache.py:41-42, 73, 79`**\n`FileSystemBytecodeCache` writes compiled template code with `pickle.dump` and reads it back with `pickle.load` (falling back to `marshal.load`). The cache file is selected purely by a hashed filename inside a caller-supplied cache directory (`cache_timeout`/`directory`). No integrity/HMAC check, no owner/permission validation, and the cache dir is frequently pointed at shared locations (`/tmp`, app-wide dirs, containers with writable volumes). Anyone able to create/write a file in that directory can plant a malicious pickle and obtain arbitrary code execution in the server process on the next `load()`. Cache poisoning is a classic escalation vector when the template directory is shared or the cache path is world-writable.\n\n**2. [High / CWE-1336, CWE-94, CWE-693] Sandbox attribute-access enforcement — `src/jinja2/sandbox.py:302, 319` (`getattr` with non-literal attribute name) and `filters.py:1183, 1426` (`do_attr`)**\nThe sandbox gatekeeping relies on `is_safe_attribute` / `is_internal_attribute` consulted through dynamic `getattr(obj, attr)` on attacker-influenced attribute names, reached from template expressions and from the `|attr` filter. In the 3.1.5 code line, dynamic `getattr` in the sandbox and the `attr` filter are the exact surface of the sandbox-escape class fixed in Jinja 3.1.6 (CVE-2025-27516): the `|attr` filter combined with internal-attribute checks allowed reaching dunder attributes (`__globals__`, `__class__`, `__init__`) and thereby escaping the sandbox to arbitrary Python execution. Because the check is name-based and applied at attribute *lookup* time rather than being enforced structurally, any name reaching the check that is not explicitly blocked (e.g. attributes of objects whose `is_internal_attribute` categorization is incomplete, or `format`/`format_map` on `str` as in CVE-2024-56326) yields code execution. Note also that `SandboxedEnvironment` is documented as not a security boundary against fully untrusted templates.\n\n**3. [High / CWE-94] Dynamic code execution of template-derived source — `debug.py:105-107, 145` and `environment.py:709, 1228`**\nTemplate source is compiled to Python with `compile()`/`exec()` and the debug `rewrite_traceback_stack` / `get_template` path re-execs reconstructed code frames. This is by design for a template engine, but it means any path where template text (not context data) is attacker-influenced — user-editable templates, database-stored templates, `Template(user_input)`, template inheritance/`{% include %}` from untrusted loaders — is direct SSTI/RCE. Flagged because it makes the boundary enforcement in (2) the only barrier for the sandbox use case.\n\n**4. [Medium / CWE-706/CWE-94] Dynamic import & attribute indirection — `loaders.py:323, 676`, `environment.py:480, 490`, `utils.py:158`, `visitor.py:33`, `nodes.py:62/165/249/265`, `parser.py:176`**\n`ModuleLoader` performs `importlib.import_module()` on a module name and `getattr`-based loader resolution; `Environment.getattr/getitem` and the AST visitor dispatch are also dynamic. These are internal/design usages (module name and loader object normally come from the application, not the template), so they only become exploitable if an application forwards untrusted strings into loader selection or environment configuration.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe package is a library with an intentionally powerful code-execution core. The concrete defects to report are (1) pickle-based bytecode cache with no integrity/ownership validation, and (2) name-based sandbox attribute filtering on `getattr`/`|attr`, which is the known sandbox-escape surface for this release line. Findings 3 and 4 are design characteristics that are only exploitable through application misuse (untrusted template source or untrusted loader configuration).",
